--TEST--
Core\Str::codePoints answers scalar values where ord answers a byte, Core\Str::fromCodePoint encodes a code point where chr wraps one modulo 256, and the member refuses the surrogate range and everything past U+10FFFF where chr answers anyway
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Two divergences, one per member, and they are the same divergence read from both ends: **PHP's callable pair works in bytes and Novis's works in scalar values**. `Core\Str::codePoints` is `nvs_stdlib::granularity::Unit::CodePoint` — deliberately *not* the class default, this being the one member whose job is to answer numbers rather than text — so `codePoints("é")` is `[233]`, where `array_map("ord", str_split("é"))` is `[195, 169]`, `ord` reading one byte and `str_split` cutting between them. `Core\Str::fromCodePoint(233)` is the two bytes `c3a9`; `chr(233)` is the single byte `e9`, which is not well-formed UTF-8 on its own and is therefore not a value Novis can hold at all (ADR 0009 § 1). The honest twins are `mb_ord`/`mb_str_split` and `mb_chr`, and none of them is callable on this leg — the `php` on the Windows `PATH` has no `mbstring` — which is the second reason the expectation below is frozen rather than computed. **`chr` has no failure mode and that is the divergence that matters.** It constrains its argument with `% 256`, so `chr(55296)`, `chr(1114112)` and `chr(0)` are all the same byte and `chr(1114111)` is `ff`: past 255 the function is not injective, and a program that hands it a code point gets a byte with no diagnostic. `Core\Str::fromCodePoint` refuses instead — a surrogate in `55296..=57343` and anything past `1114111` are a catchable `Fault::thrown` (`nvs_stdlib::str::scalar_value`), never a substituted U+FFFD, because ADR 0009 § 3's rule that a lossy conversion is checked rather than repaired applies to a code point exactly as it does to a buffer. PHP now agrees that the wrap was a mistake and says so at the other end: on 8.5.9 `chr()` **deprecates** an argument outside `0..255`, so the row this file freezes is one PHP is already retreating from. The port of a program that genuinely wanted a byte says so over `bytes`, where the operation belongs, rather than through a member named for text. **What is not a divergence is the round trip**: `codePoints` and `fromCodePoint` invert each other on every subject below, cluster-splitting ones included, exactly as they do over the ASCII table in the companion case, `str-code-points-and-from-code-point-over-ascii-match-str_split-ord-and-chr`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 1's `codePoints` and `fromCodePoint` rows, over subjects where the
// byte, the code point and the grapheme cluster are three different counts.
// The agreeing half — the whole 0..127 table against `chr`, the round trip
// counted on all 128, and `str_split` plus `array_map("ord", …)` against
// `codePoints` — is in the companion file, whose name is the same as this
// one's with `over-ascii-match-str_split-ord-and-chr` in place of
// `past-ascii-diverge-from-ord-and-chr`.

final class Show {
    // A case cannot echo a `bytes` (ADR 0009 § 3) and must never freeze a
    // decomposed cluster into an `--EXPECT--` block — `e` followed by
    // U+0301 and the single U+00E9 are different strings that no editor
    // renders differently — so every subject here is shown as its bytes.
    public static function bytes(string $text): string {
        return Core\Encoding::toHex($text as bytes);
    }
}

// Seven subjects, one per way the three units can disagree. `é`, `中` and
// `👍` are one code point and one cluster in two, three and four bytes, so
// they part from `ord`/`str_split` and from nothing else. `e` + U+0301 and
// `👍` + U+1F3FD are each two code points in one cluster — a combining mark
// and an emoji modifier, UAX #29's two commonest reasons a cluster is not a
// code point. `aé中👍` has one code point per cluster and four different
// byte widths. `\r\n` is the one place *inside* ASCII where the class
// default and this member's unit part company: two code points, one cluster.
array<string> $subjects = ["é", "e\u{0301}", "中", "👍", "👍\u{1F3FD}", "aé中👍", "\r\n"];

// Per subject: its bytes, its scalar values, how many of those there are,
// and how many grapheme clusters `Core\Str::length` counts. The scalar
// values go through `Core\Json::encode` because that is the only spelling
// that renders an `array<uint>` in one piece. Reading the four columns
// across is the whole divergence: the first is what PHP's `ord` and
// `str_split` see, the second is what this member answers, and the third and
// fourth are why `codePoints` is not `Core\Str::graphemes` under another
// name.
foreach ($subjects as string $subject) {
    echo Show::bytes($subject), " ",
        Core\Json::encode(Core\Str::codePoints($subject)), " ",
        Core\Arr::count(Core\Str::codePoints($subject)), " ",
        Core\Str::length($subject), "\n";
}

// The same table as a count, because the point is that the two units differ
// on *some* subjects and not on all of them: a member that answered clusters
// would score seven here and a member that answered code points scores four.
// That number is the member's whole reason to exist beside `graphemes`
// (`nvs_core_str_code_points`'s doc comment).
int $agree = 0;
int $cells = 0;
foreach ($subjects as string $one) {
    $cells = $cells + 1;
    if (Core\Arr::count(Core\Str::codePoints($one)) == Core\Str::length($one)) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
}
echo $agree, " of ", $cells, "\n";

// The round trip survives the unit change, both ways round, on all seven —
// including the two subjects whose clusters this member splits. Rebuilding
// one code point at a time is the composition that ties the two members
// named here to each other with nothing in between; going through
// `fromCodePoints` is the array-shaped spelling of the same thing.
int $back = 0;
int $piece = 0;
foreach ($subjects as string $each) {
    if (Core\Str::fromCodePoints(Core\Str::codePoints($each)) == $each) {
        $back = $back + 1;
    }
    string $out = "";
    foreach (Core\Str::codePoints($each) as uint $point) {
        $out = $out . Core\Str::fromCodePoint($point);
    }
    if ($out == $each) {
        $piece = $piece + 1;
    }
}
echo $back, " ", $piece, " of ", $cells, "\n";

// The bound, both sides of it, three times over. `chr` accepts every one of
// these ten and answers a byte for each; `Core\Str::fromCodePoint` accepts
// seven and throws on three, and the three are named in pairs with their
// neighbours so a member stopping one entry early or late is visible:
//
// * 127 and 128 — the last code point that is one byte and the first that is
//   two, which is exactly where the companion case's oracle ends.
// * 55295 (U+D7FF) and 55296 (U+D800) — the last scalar value below the
//   surrogate block and its first member; then 57343 (U+DFFF) and 57344
//   (U+E000), the last surrogate and the first scalar value above it.
// * 1114111 (U+10FFFF) and 1114112 — the highest code point Unicode has and
//   the first that does not exist.
//
// 233 and 65533 are ordinary rows for contrast: U+FFFD is in the table
// because it is what a repairing conversion would have substituted, and this
// member never substitutes it (ADR 0009 § 3).
array<int> $probes = [127, 128, 233, 55295, 55296, 57343, 57344, 65533, 1114111, 1114112];
int $made = 0;
int $threw = 0;
foreach ($probes as int $probe) {
    try {
        string $built = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($probe as uint);
        echo "[", Show::bytes($built), "]";
        $made = $made + 1;
    } catch (Throwable $refused) {
        echo "[refused]";
        $threw = $threw + 1;
    }
}
echo "\n", $made, " built, ", $threw, " refused\n";
--EXPECT--
c3a9 [233] 1 1
65cc81 [101,769] 2 1
e4b8ad [20013] 1 1
f09f918d [128077] 1 1
f09f918df09f8fbd [128077,127997] 2 1
61c3a9e4b8adf09f918d [97,233,20013,128077] 4 4
0d0a [13,10] 2 1
4 of 7
7 7 of 7
[7f][c280][c3a9][ed9fbf][refused][refused][ee8080][efbfbd][f48fbfbf][refused]
7 built, 3 refused
